Name: []

Type: keyword

Syntax: []

Summary:
The square bracket characters [ ] surround the <element> name in an
<array> reference, and the values of a literal array.

Introduced: 1.0

OS: mac, windows, linux, android

Platforms: desktop, server, mobile

Example:
put thisValue into myArray["someKey"]

Example:
if myArray["someOtherKey"] is "A" then beep

Example:
put ["red", "green", "blue"] into tColors -- tColors[2] is "green"

Example:
put {"name": "Ada", "born": 1815} into tPerson

Description:
Use square brackets [ ] to specify which <element> in an <array> you are
referring to.

You access an element of an array by using the name of the array, along
with the key in square brackets:

    put "A" into myList["firstLetter"]
    put "B" into myList["secondLetter"]
    put "C" into myList["thirdLetter"]
    get myList["secondLetter"] -- yields "B"


Square brackets must be used in pairs, each [ with a matching ]. Using a
single square bracket does not cause an <error>, but the bracket is
interpreted as part of the <variable> name, rather than as the marker
for the <key> of an <array> reference.

**Literal arrays:** Where an expression goes, square brackets around a
list of values, with commas between them, make an <array> whose keys are
1, 2, 3 and on, in the order the values are written. Curly brackets
around a list of elements, each a key, a colon and a value, make an
array of those elements:

    put [10, "two", 1 + 2] into tList -- tList[3] is 3
    put {"name": "Ada", "born": 1815} into tPerson
    put {"list": [1, 2], "map": {"x": "y"}} into tData
    put [] into tEmpty -- an array with no elements

Each key and each value is an expression, worked out each time the
literal is reached, so that a literal gives a new array each time. A
comma may follow the last element, but every element must be written
out: `[1,,2]` is an error. A key given twice gives its element the later
value, keys being compared as the <caseSensitive> says, and a key that
is an array with elements is an error. A literal can be written over
more than one line only with a "\" at the end of each line but the last.

A square bracket after a variable's name is still that variable's key,
with or without a space before it: `put tA [1] into tB` puts `tA[1]`
into `tB`.

>*Note:* Literal arrays are as in LiveCode 10. A script that compiled
> before means what it did: a handler, a <do> script or a
> <value(function)> expression is read with literal arrays only if it
> does not compile without them, and one that compiles neither way gives
> the error it gave before. Text that is worked out only if it compiles,
> and left as text otherwise, is never read with them: a <merge>
> template's `[[...]]` and `<?...?>`, and a parameter of <send> given as
> text. So wiki, JSON or JavaScript text there is left as it was. A
> <value(function)> or <do> of text that was an error before, such as
> `value("[1, 2]")`, now gives an array.

>*Note:* Arrays in OpenXTalk are associative (also known as hash tables).
> This means that when an array element is accessed using square bracket
> notation, the value in between the brackets is used to "look up" the
> element, and is not a numeric index as in C, Pascal and other
> languages. Of course it is still possible to use numbers as array
> keys, but OpenXTalk internally converts these to strings before
> retrieving the array elements.

References: split (command), do (command), send (command),
arrayEncode (function), merge (function), value (function),
caseSensitive (property), array (glossary), variable (glossary),
error (glossary), key (glossary), element (keyword), () (operator)

Tags: properties

